\description{
This function checks, \strong{a posteriori}, that each taxon grafted on a phylogeny sits in a valid position.
For missing taxa grafted with \code{graft_missing_taxa()}: within its target clade (or on its stem branch if allowed), and outside its excluded subclades.
For missing clades grafted with \code{graft_missing_clades()}: each missing clade (the taxa sharing the same sister clade) must form one clade, grafted on the stem branch of its sister clade.

The type of grafting is detected from \code{missing_taxa_list}: a \verb{$Target_clade} item for taxa, a \verb{$Sister_clade} item for missing clades (see 'Missing clades' in Details section).
With a \code{simmap}, the validity of the states of the taxa or of the missing clades are checked too.

The function can also \strong{check that the backbone phylogeny is preserved} by the grafting once the grafted taxa are pruned.
}
\details{
\subsection{Missing taxa}{

\strong{A taxon respects its target clade if it is found within the clade, or on its stem branch if the stem is accepted for this taxon}:
global \code{include_target_clade_stem = TRUE}, or \code{missing_taxa_list$include_target_clade_stem} of this taxon
(the taxon is then sister to the target clade, or to other missing taxa sister to the target clade, with no other backbone tip in between).
For a single-tip target clade, the taxon must be on the branch of the tip, with no other backbone tip in between.

The 'stem' position is not restricted to a taxon being directly sister to the target clade: \strong{taxa grafted on the stem can receive further grafts},
so several missing taxa can form together a group sister to the target clade (i.e, a \strong{missing-only subtree} grafted on the the stem).
Only backbone tips are considered when checking the position of a taxon (missing taxa are ignored), so such configurations are valid.

If \code{phy_grafted} has a \verb{$simmap} item (the updated \code{simmap} returned by the grafting functions when a \code{simmap} is provided), the states are also checked:
a grafted taxon is in the state of the point where it was grafted, along its whole terminal branch, so the states of its terminal branch in the \code{simmap} must be allowed.
The \code{simmap} item is used as it is: it is not compared to the backbone.

\strong{A taxon respects its excluded subclades if it is not nested within any of them.}
If the stem of an excluded subclade is excluded (\code{exclude_subclades_stem = TRUE}, the default), the taxon must also not be on its stem, i.e., sister to the subclade
(or to other missing taxa sister to the subclade), with no other backbone tip in between. For a single-tip excluded subclade, the taxon must not be on the branch of the tip.
As in \code{\link[=graft_missing_taxa]{phylograftR::graft_missing_taxa()}}, if the stem of the subclade is not excluded, the taxon can be sister to the subclade, not within it.

Branches of the backbone are matched between the pruned grafted phylogeny and the backbone by the set of tips they subtend.
Branch lengths are considered preserved if the maximum deviation, relative to the depth of the backbone, is lower than \code{tolerance}.
}

\subsection{Missing clades}{

\code{graft_missing_clades()} grafts the taxa sharing the same sister clade together, as \strong{one missing clade}, on the stem branch of their sister clade.
With a \verb{$Sister_clade} list, this function checks each missing clade (the taxa whose sister clades have the same backbone tips, whatever the pair of tips used to define them) for:
\itemize{
\item its presence (\verb{$in_tree}): all its taxa must be in the grafted phylogeny ;
\item its monophyly (\verb{$monophyletic}): its taxa must form one clade of the grafted phylogeny, free of any other tip ;
\item its position (\verb{$sister_position} and \verb{$sister_ok}): the missing clade must be sister to its sister clade, or to other missing taxa grafted on the same stem, with no other backbone tip in between (\code{"stem"}), or on the terminal branch of a single-tip sister clade (\code{"tip_branch"}).
A missing clade nested within its sister clade (\code{"crown"}) is a violation, unlike a taxon grafted by \code{graft_missing_taxa()} within its target clade;
\item with a \verb{$simmap} item, its states (\verb{$states_ok}): the whole missing clade is in the state of the point where it was grafted, so its stem branch and all its descendant branches must have one single state,
which must be allowed. The taxa of a missing clade are grafted together, so they must share their allowed states (the function stops otherwise, as the grafting function does).
With \code{treat_states_as_ranges = TRUE}, the whole missing clade inherits one range, which must be contained in the state of the lineage at the graft point (see 'Biogeographic ranges as states').
}

*e arguments \code{include_target_clade_stem}, \code{use_excluded_subclades} and \code{exclude_subclades_stem} only apply to taxa and are ignored. The backbone is checked as for taxa.
Several phylogenies, maps and traits are handled as described in the corresponding sections. With \code{verbose = TRUE}, the summary counts missing clades instead of taxa.
}

\subsection{Several traits}{

If the grafting used several traits as constraints, the grafting functions return a \verb{$simmap} item that is a list of simmaps, one per trait, named after the traits,
(or \code{"trait_1"}, \code{"trait_2"}, ... if the input was not named). Then this function checks the validity of states trait by trait:
the states of the terminal branch of the taxon in the simmap of each trait must be allowed for this trait.
The allowed states of each taxon and trait are resolved as in the grafting functions: the value of \code{missing_taxa_list$allowed_states} for this trait if provided,
otherwise the global \code{allowed_states} for this trait, otherwise all the states of the trait (see \code{\link[=graft_missing_taxa]{phylograftR::graft_missing_taxa()}}).
\verb{$states_ok_<trait>} gives the result for each trait, and \strong{\verb{$states_ok} is \code{TRUE} only if all traits are ok}.
With \code{verbose = TRUE}, the number of violations is also given for each trait.

With a trait treated as ranges (\code{treat_states_as_ranges}, one \code{TRUE} in the vector), the checks of 'Biogeographic ranges as states' are performed on this trait:
\verb{$states_ok_<trait>} is \code{TRUE} if the range inherited is one of the allowed ranges (any range if the taxon has no allowed ranges),
and \verb{$ranges_ok} is \code{TRUE} if the range inherited is contained in the state of the lineage of this trait at the graft point.
The other traits are checked as exact matches. The attribute \code{"range_trait"} gives the name of the range trait.
}

\subsection{Several grafted phylogenies}{

The phylogenies are checked one by one against the same \code{phy_backbone} and \code{missing_taxa_list}. When \code{allowed_states} or \code{treat_states_as_ranges} are used,
each phylogeny is checked with its own \verb{$simmap} item (the maps of a \code{"multiSimmap"} can differ). The warnings about the inputs are sent once.
With several phylogenies, \code{verbose = TRUE} prints one summary for all of them (number of phylogenies respecting all the constraints, number of violations of each type,
and the first phylogenies with violations), and \code{stop_on_failure = TRUE} stops if any phylogeny violates a constraint.
}

\subsection{Biogeographic ranges as states}{

With \code{treat_states_as_ranges = TRUE}, the states are biogeographic ranges (one capital letter per area, e.g., \code{"A"}, \code{"AB"}, \code{"BC"}), and a range is a set of areas.
A grafted lineage inherits one of its allowed ranges, which can be a subset of the state of the lineage it is grafted on.
Example: the range \code{"A"} can be inherited from a lineage in the state \code{"AB"}. This then describes a subset sympatric speciation event: \verb{(AB) -> (AB), (A)}.

Two checks are then performed:
\itemize{
\item \verb{$states_ok}: the state of the terminal branch of the taxon is exactly one of its allowed ranges (the range inherited).
\item \verb{$ranges_ok}: the range inherited is contained in the state of the lineage at the graft point (e.g., \code{"A"} is contained in \code{"AB"}, but \code{"BC"} is not).
The check is skipped (\code{NA}) for taxa grafted at the root.
}
}
}
\examples{
# --- Prepare data --- #

# Create a toy backbone phylogeny: ultrametric, with a root age of 50 time units
set.seed(1234)
phy <- ape::rcoal(n = 30)
phy$edge.length <- phy$edge.length / max(ape::node.depth.edgelength(phy)) * 50

# List the missing taxa to graft
missing_taxa_list <- list(
  Taxon_name = c("X1", "X2", "X3", "X4"),
  # Items are named after Taxon_name (same names, same order)
  Target_clade = list(X1 = c("t26", "t18"), X2 = c("t8", "t22"), X3 = "t5", X4 = c("t26", "t18")),
  Excluded_subclades = list(X1 = list(c("t10", "t19")), X2 = NULL, X3 = NULL, X4 = NULL))

# --- Perform grafting of missing taxa--- #

# Graft the missing taxa with graft_missing_taxa()
grafted_phy <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list,
                                  verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# --- Check one grafted phylogenies --- #

# Check that the grafted taxa respect their constraints, and that the backbone is preserved
check_results <- check_grafting_constraints(phy_grafted = grafted_phy, phy_backbone = phy,
                                            missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list)
# Inspect results
check_results
# Check overall results
attr(check_results, "all_ok")

# --- Check several grafted phylogenies --- #

# Build 3 grafted phylognies (i.e., simulations) with the same backbone and constraints
grafted_phys <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list,
                                   nb_simulations = 3, verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# Check all resulting grafted phylogenies at once
check_results_multi <- check_grafting_constraints(phy_grafted = grafted_phys, phy_backbone = phy,
                                                  missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list)

# With several simulations: the data.frame has the columns 'map', 'simulation' and 'seed',
# and the summary is printed once for all the phylogenies
head(check_results_multi[, c("map", "simulation", "seed", "Taxon_name", "ok")])

# An additional attribute provide a summary per phylogeny:
# number of taxa that failed, backbone preserved, all constraints respected
attr(check_results_multi, "phylogenies")


# --- Check missing clades grafted with graft_missing_clades() --- #

# With a 'Sister_clade' item, the missing clades are checked (one row per missing clade)

# Prepare a missing_taxa_list for missing clades
missing_clades_list <- list(
  Taxon_name = c("M1", "M2", "M3"),
  Sister_clade = list(M1 = c("t26", "t18"), M2 = c("t26", "t18"), M3 = "t5"))

# Graft the missing clades with graft_missing_clades()
grafted_clades <- graft_missing_clades(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_clades_list,
                                       verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# Check results
check_clades <- check_grafting_constraints(phy_grafted = grafted_clades, phy_backbone = phy,
                                           missing_taxa_list = missing_clades_list)
# Inspect results
check_clades[, c("missing_clade", "Taxon_names", "monophyletic", "sister_position", "ok")]


# --- Check the constraints from several traits, with biogeographic ranges --- #

# With the stochastic maps of several traits used to assign grafting constraints,
# 'allowed_states' has one element per trait,
# and 'treat_states_as_ranges' is a logical vector with one value per trait
# used to identify which trait was treated as biogeographic ranges.

# Load datasets
data(toy_backbone)
data(toy_simmap_ranges)
data(toy_simmap_elevation)
data(toy_missing_taxa_list_with_ranges_and_elevation)

# Graft phylogeny using elevation and biogeographic ranges as constraints
grafted_phy_traits <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = toy_backbone,
    missing_taxa_list = toy_missing_taxa_list_with_ranges_and_elevation,
    simmap = list(biogeo = toy_simmap_ranges, elevation = toy_simmap_elevation),
    allowed_states = list(c("A", "B"), NULL),
    treat_states_as_ranges = c(biogeo = TRUE, elevation = FALSE),
    verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# The result has the joint columns 'states_ok' and 'ranges_ok' (ranges of the 'range trait'),
# and the column 'states_ok_<trait>' for each trait. The summary details the failures per trait.
check_traits <- check_grafting_constraints(
    phy_grafted = grafted_phy_traits,
    phy_backbone = toy_backbone,
    missing_taxa_list = toy_missing_taxa_list_with_ranges_and_elevation,
    allowed_states = list(c("A", "B"), NULL),
    treat_states_as_ranges = c(biogeo = TRUE, elevation = FALSE))
check_traits[, c("Taxon_name", "states_ok", "states_ok_biogeo", "states_ok_elevation",
                 "ranges_ok", "ok")]


}
